# Dev Log

## 2026-09-12 — FBref blocked by Cloudflare; pivoted stats to API-Football

**What:** Confirmed FBref is behind a Cloudflare JavaScript challenge
(the "Just a moment..." page), not just a basic bot-header check.
Switched the stats source to API-Football, kept `db_utils.py`
completely unchanged, and separately confirmed Transfermarkt has no
such protection — a plain `requests.Session()` with a homepage
warm-up gets a clean 200. Built and offline-tested
`scraper/transfermarkt_scraper.py` against Transfermarkt's real
markup (no `data-stat` attributes here — structural/positional
parsing instead, with player name+position nested inside an
inline-table per row).

**Why pivot instead of pushing through with Playwright:** A headless
browser can solve a JS challenge locally, but GitHub Actions' cloud
runners share IP ranges Cloudflare already treats with more
suspicion than a residential IP — so it likely wouldn't run reliably
unattended anyway. Since the whole point of this project is
automated freshness, I picked reliability over sticking with FBref.
API-Football's free tier (100 req/day) comfortably covers a full
league pull (~28 requests, paginated 20/page).

**Why db_utils.py didn't need to change:** It was written to expect
a generic `{name, nationality, position, stats}` shape, not anything
FBref-specific. Persistence being separate from fetching/parsing
meant swapping data sources was a new file, not a rewrite.

**What I learned:** A 403 isn't always "bot detected" — it can mean
several different things (missing headers, rate limiting, or a full
JS challenge), and the fix is different for each. The diagnostic
step (checking response body content, not just status code) is what
told them apart here. Also: don't assume two sites with similar
purposes have similar bot protection — testing each independently
mattered.

**Open question / next up:** Entity resolution between API-Football
and Transfermarkt player IDs — same problem as before, different
source pair. Also need to confirm whether Transfermarkt's "top
market values" page covers full squads by position or just the
highest-valued subset league-wide, which affects whether it's
sufficient on its own or needs per-club squad pages instead.

