## 2026-09-08 — Schema + FBref scraper

**What:** Finalized the DB schema (long/tidy stats table, separate
valuations table, `source_ids` for cross-site entity resolution) and
built the FBref scraper for Premier League standard stats. Split it
into `scraper/fbref_scraper.py` (fetch + parse) and
`scraper/db_utils.py` (persistence), orchestrated from `main.py`.

**Why long/tidy over wide:** A goalkeeper's stats and a striker's
stats barely overlap. Wide columns meant mostly-NULL rows and a
schema migration every time I wanted to track a new stat. Long/tidy
trades query convenience for flexibility — I pivot to wide only at
feature-engineering time, right before modeling.

**Why snapshots instead of overwrite:** Overwriting loses history.
The whole "stays fresh for transfer windows" claim only works if I
can show a value/stat trend over time, not just a single current
number.

**Why the db file is committed, not gitignored:** The automation
story only proves itself if the scheduled scrape visibly updates the
repo. A gitignored database means the freshness is invisible.

**What I learned:** FBref tags every stat cell with a `data-stat`
attribute, which sidesteps fragile header-text parsing entirely.
Also learned some FBref tables are hidden inside HTML comments
because they're normally rendered client-side — first time I've had
to scrape around that.

**Open question / next up:** Entity resolution between FBref and
Transfermarkt IDs — different sites, no shared key, name spellings
won't always match exactly.

